Hecks.hecksagon "Threading" do
  # Threading wiring — the adapter that drives the spawn syscall on
  # Thread.SpawnRequested. Bluebook stays pure (records the intent +
  # emits) ; this adapter is the impure leaf where the runtime calls
  # tokio::spawn / std::thread::spawn.
  #
  # Mirrors the ProcessHealth arc : Sweep + Heal record on the
  # bluebook ; Primitive::Process.Spawn is the syscall leaf the
  # hecksagon binds. Here the leaf IS the spawn syscall itself, so
  # the rust actor module (rust/src/runtime/actor/registry.rs)
  # treats Threading.Thread.Spawn as the canonical entry point and
  # the substrate dispatch (tokio task vs std::thread) lives behind
  # the kind attribute.
  #
  # ── Driven on Thread.SpawnRequested ──
  #
  # When a policy or the actor registry dispatches Threading.Thread.Spawn,
  # the bluebook emits SpawnRequested. The adapter is the runtime hook
  # that : (a) looks up the kind, (b) calls the matching syscall
  # (tokio::spawn for kind=tokio, std::thread::spawn for kind=std),
  # (c) stashes the real handle in a side-table keyed by Thread.name,
  # (d) dispatches Thread.Drain back through the bus once the substrate
  # accepted the task so the bluebook reflects state=running, and
  # (e) on completion dispatches Thread.Join (happy path) or
  # Thread.MarkPoisoned (supervisor caught a panic).
  #
  # Sister event Mailbox.MailboxDraining is the runtime audit trail of
  # the actor registry's drain_all_in_parallel walk (the Mailbox-side
  # command is named StartDraining to avoid colliding with Thread.Drain
  # in the same bluebook) — same adapter,
  # different leaf (the mailbox queue drain). Both events fan out
  # through this hecksagon today as :memory while the rust side still
  # owns the spawn / drain syscalls inline ; the adapter is the
  # declared seam where the recursive bus-through-self binding lands
  # next (deferred from sprint 14 ; see threading.bluebook vision).
  #
  # cwd contract : tokio tasks inherit the runtime process cwd ;
  # std::thread spawns inherit the same. No script invocation here —
  # the syscall is a Rust function call, not a process spawn, so the
  # exec_dispatcher path (Primitive::Process.Spawn) is NOT reused.

  adapter :memory
end
